## TypeScript API for 3D Camera Animation (Prioritizing Helpfulness & Verbosity)

This API aims to provide a user-friendly, robust, and expressive way to control 3D camera animations in TypeScript, balancing simplicity with granular control.

**Core Concepts:**

1. **AnimationBlueprint:**  The foundation is the `AnimationBlueprint` class, representing a complete camera movement sequence. It holds:
   - **Timeline:**  A structured representation (e.g., keyframes) defining camera parameters over time. Keyframes can encompass:
     - `position` (Vec3), `rotation` (Euler angles or Quaternions), `fov`, `near/far clipping planes`, and optional custom properties.
   - **Easing Functions:**  Built-in and customizable easing functions (`easeInQuad`, `easeOutBounce`, etc.) to control interpolation smoothness.
   - **Duration & Playback Options:**  Total animation length, loop settings (ping-pong, once, reverse), and playback speed control.

2. **CameraController Interface:**  An abstraction layer interacting with your 3D engine/renderer (Three.js, Babylon.js, etc.).  Developers **implement** this interface, providing engine-specific methods:
   - `applyBlueprint(blueprint: AnimationBlueprint)`: Executes the animation smoothly on the connected camera.
   - `getCurrentState()`: Retrieves real-time camera data for dynamic adjustments within animations.

**API Structure (TypeScript Definitions):**

```typescript
import { Vec3, Quaternion } // Assume common vector/rotation types from your engine

interface EasingFunction {
  (t: number, b: number, c: number, d: number): number;
}

class Keyframe {
  position: Vec3;
  rotation: Quaternion | EulerAngles; // Engine-specific rotation type
  fov: number;
  near: number;
  far: number;
  // ... additional custom property support
  time: number; // Position on the timeline
  easing: EasingFunction; // Applied to this keyframe's interpolation
}

class AnimationBlueprint {
  keyframes: Keyframe[];
  duration: number;
  loopType: 'once' | 'loop' | 'pingPong';
  playbackSpeed: number;
  easingGlobal: EasingFunction; // Overall blueprint easing

  constructor(keyframes: Keyframe[], options: { duration: number, ... })

## TypeScript End-User API for 3D Camera Animation (Prioritizing Helpfulness & Verbosity)

This API aims to be intuitive, robust, and  TypeScript-friendly for controlling 3D camera animations, balancing ease of use with expressive power.

**Core Concepts & Structure:**

1. **`CameraController` Class:** The central hub managing animation logic. Instances represent individual cameras.

   ```typescript
   class CameraController {
     constructor(scene: THREE.Scene, initialPosition: THREE.Vector3, target?: THREE.Object3D) {
       this.scene = scene;
       this.camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(/* ... setup with initialPosition, target */);  // Internally managed
       this.animationState = new AnimationState(); // Holds active animations
     }

     // Animation Definition & Playback
     addAnimation(name: string, animation: AnimationDefinition): this {
       this.animationState.add(name, animation);
       return this; // Chainable for fluent API
     }

     play(name: string, options?: { loop?: boolean, speed?: number }): this {
       this.animationState.play(name, options);
       return this;
     }
     pause(name: string): this {
       this.animationState.pause(name);
       return this;
     }
     stop(name: string): this {
       this.animationState.stop(name);
       return this;
     }

     // Camera Access & Manipulation (Direct Control)
     setPosition(position: THREE.Vector3): this {
       this.camera.position.copy(position);
       return this;
     }
     lookAt(target: THREE.Object3D | THREE.Vector3): this {
       this.camera.lookAt(target);
       return this;
     }

     // Utility & Synchronization
     update(): void { this.animationState.update(); } //  Call in your scene loop
   }
   ```

2. **`AnimationDefinition` Interface:**  Blueprint for describing animations.

   ```typescript
   interface AnimationDefinition {
     duration: number; // Seconds
     keyframes: Keyframe[]; // Array of poses (position, rotation, FOV, etc.)
     easing?: (t: number) => number; // Custom easing function (optional)

