**1. Hardware Setup**

* **NodeMCU:**  Your ESP8266 development board.
* **LED Strip:** A 60-LED addressable RGB strip (WS2812B or similar).  Ensure it connects to data (DIN) and power pins on your NodeMCU.
* **Button:**  A momentary push button connected to a digital input pin on the NodeMCU (e.g., GPIO0).  Add a pull-up resistor (around 10k ohms) to this pin for proper button reading.

**2.  Code Structure (Arduino IDE)**

```c++
#include <FastLED.h> // Essential library for addressable LEDs

// Define LED Strip Configuration
#define NUM_LEDS 60
CRGB leds[NUM_LEDS]; // Array to store LED colors
#define DATA_PIN 5   // Modify if your data pin differs

FastLED.addLeds<WS2812B, DATA_PIN, GRB>(leds, NUM_LEDS); // Initialize FastLED

// Button Input
const int buttonPin = 0; // Change if using a different GPIO
int lastButtonPressed = 0; // Track previous press for debouncing

void setup() {
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP); // Internal pull-up resistor
  FastLED.setBrightness(80); // Adjust overall LED brightness (0-255)
  Serial.begin(115200); // For debugging (optional)
}

void loop() {
  // Button Debouncing (prevents multiple triggers from a single press)
